# Pipes and redirection
> `>` sends a command's output to a file instead of the window, as in AmigaDOS.
> `>>` adds to the end of the file. `<` reads a file as input. `|` (a pipe) hands the
> output of one command to the next, so small tools combine.
$ printf 'pear\napple\nfig\napple\n' > fruit.txt
? Write the sorted list of fruit.txt into sorted.txt.
= [ "$(head -n 1 sorted.txt)" = apple ] && [ "$(wc -l < sorted.txt | tr -d ' ')" = 4 ]
~ sort fruit.txt > sorted.txt
! sort fruit.txt > sorted.txt
? Add the line plum to the end of fruit.txt.
= [ "$(tail -n 1 fruit.txt)" = plum ]
~ echo plum >> fruit.txt (two > signs add, one > replaces).
! echo plum >> fruit.txt
? Count the lines of fruit.txt with wc, reading the file with <.
= case "$LEARN_LINE" in *wc*"<"*) true ;; *) false ;; esac
= [ "$(tr -d ' ' < "$LEARN_OUT")" = 5 ]
~ wc -l < fruit.txt
! wc -l < fruit.txt
? Count how many lines of fruit.txt hold apple, with grep and a pipe into wc.
= case "$LEARN_LINE" in *grep*"|"*wc*) true ;; *) false ;; esac
= [ "$(tr -d ' ' < "$LEARN_OUT")" = 2 ]
~ grep apple fruit.txt | wc -l
! grep apple fruit.txt | wc -l
